      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "Given that the pressure (P) is inversely proportional to the volume (V), it can be mathematically represented as \\( P \\propto \\frac{1}{V} \\). This intuition implies that if the volume of a gas is increased while keeping the temperature constant, both its pressure and volume should decrease proportionally inversely, following the formula: \n\\[ P_1V_1 = P_2V_2 \\]\nWhere \\( P_1 \\) and \\( V_1 \\) are the initial pressure and volume, and \\( P_2 \\) and \\( V_2 \\) are the final pressure and volume.\n\nIn this problem, initially, the oxygen is in a 2.28-liter container with a pressure of 5 kPa. Therefore, the product of the initial pressure and volume is:\n\\[ P_1V_1 = 5 \\, \\text{kPa} \\times 2.28 \\, \\text{liter} \\]\n\nWhen all the oxygen is moved to a 5.7-liter container at the same temperature, the new volume is 5.7 liters, and let \\( P_2 \\) be the new pressure. Using the formula for inverse variation:\n\\[ P_1V_1 = P_2V_2 \\]\n\nSubstituting the known values:\n\\[ 5 \\, \\text{kPa} \\times 2.28 \\, \\text{liter} = P_2 \\times 5.7 \\, \\text{liter} \\]\n\nSolving for \\( P_2 \\):\n\\[ P_2 = \\frac{5 \\, \\text{kPa} \\times 2.28 \\, \\text{liter}}{5.7 \\, \\text{liter}} \\]\n\\[ P_2 = \\frac{11.4 \\, \\text{kPa} \\cdot \\text{liter}}{5.7 \\, \\text{liter}} \\]\n\\[ P_2 \\approx 2 \\, \\text{kPa} \\]\n\nTherefore, the new pressure of the oxygen gas is approximately \\(\\boxed{2}\\) kPa, when converted to scientific notation, it becomes \\( P_2 \\approx 2 \\times 10^0 \\) kPa.",
